 Harandi, 2024 ) . DUO ( Park et al., 2024 ) optimizes preferences, EraseFlow ( Kusumba et al., 2025 ) a trajectory-balance criterion, and EraseDiff ( Wu et al., 2025 ) a bi-level problem. Note that where the methods above regress, they use the squared distance. Sampling-time methods leave the weights unchanged. They rewrite the prompt ( Ni et al., 2024 ) , change the guidance ( Schramowski et al., 2023 ; Jain et al., 2024 ) , project the text embeddings off an unsafe subspace ( Yoon et al., 2025 ) or add learned concept directions to the activations ( Li et al., 2024 ) . Robustness. Several attacks recover erased concepts. Ring-A-Bell ( Tsai et al., 2024 ) , P4D ( Chin et al., 2024 ) and UnlearnDiff ( Zhang et al., 2024c ) optimize the prompt. Pham et al. (2024) learn token embeddings instead, and ReFlux ( Jiang et al., 2025 ) reactivates erased concepts in FLUX. MMA-Diffusion ( Yang et al., 2024 ) bypasses prompt filters and safety checkers. Defenses train against such attacks ( Huang et al., 2024 ; Kim et al., 2024 ; Srivatsan et al., 2025 ; Zhang et al., 2024b ) or resist fine-tuning back toward the concept ( Zheng & Yeh, 2024 ; Gao et al., 2025b ) . We evaluate on the prompts of Ring-A-Bell, MMA-Diffusion and P4D but do not train against attacks. Flow models. EraseAnything ( Gao et al., 2025a ) carries the teacher-guided construction to rectified flow transformers. It places erasure and preservation in the two levels of a bi-level problem, which EraseAnything++ ( Fan et al., 2026 ) recasts as a constrained multi-objective problem. Other methods handle single-stream transformers ( Jiang et al., 2026 ) , regress the final outputs of the sampler through all steps ( Zhang et al., 2025b ) , optimize erasure and preservation rewards ( Sun et al., 2026a ) or unlearn by flow matching toward a reweighted target ( Simone et al., 2025 ) . Without training, DVE ( Zhang et al., 2026 ) projects the velocity off the direction in which the concept and anchor velocities differ, and Mosaic ( Ko et al., 2026 ) blends the velocity fields of erased models per region. The unlearning procedure we build on. GEM ( Grebe et al., 2026 ) bridges the trajectory view of EraseFlow and the teacher-guided view of ESD. From the trajectory view it takes one observation: erasure should be reinforced across a segment of the generation path, not at one noise level. It therefore fixes a window, the first t stop + 1 t_{\mathrm{stop}}+1 steps of the sampler counted from the noise, and evaluates all the velocities in it in parallel. From the teacher-guided view it takes the frozen teacher, which supplies a safe and an unsafe velocity. Its objective, however, is not a regression. Write d positive d_{\mathrm{positive}} for the distance of the student to the teacher’s velocity on the anchor prompt, and d negative d_{\mathrm{negative}} for its distance to the teacher’s velocity on the concept prompt. GEM minimizes the contrastive hinge ℒ GEM ​ ( 𝜽 ) = max ⁡ ( 0 , d positive − η ​ d negative ) , \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{GEM}}({ m{	heta}})\;=\;\max ig(0,\;d_{\mathrm{positive}}-\eta\,d_{\mathrm{negative}} ig), (7) which pulls the student toward the safe velocity and pushes it away from the unsafe one. A noise level switches off once d positive ≤ η ​ d negative d_{\mathrm{positive}}\leq\eta\,d_{\mathrm{negative}} . In contrast to these methods, BEM learns the loss. EraseAnything and EraseDiff optimize the model at both levels of their bi-level problems, DVE removes the concept direction at sampling time and GEM minimizes a hinge. The outer level of our bi-level problem instead learns the potential that defines the loss, which weights the concept direction inside a regression. We build on GEM for three reasons. First, it is a recent state-of-the-art method: among the methods it compares with, it reports the lowest unsafe rates on all nudity and gore benchmarks we use (App. A.2 ). Second, its objective is a single loss on the velocities, so the loss is what defines the method, whereas other methods for flow models add further terms, such as attention terms or constraints. GEM is therefore the natural setting in which to study the loss itself. Third, its released code reproduces the reported results closely on most benchmarks (App. A.2 ), which we did not find for all the methods we considered. We therefore keep its unlearning procedure and swap the hinge loss of Eq. ( 7 ) for a learned Bregman divergence. 4 Learning the Divergence We adopt the unlearning procedure of GEM ( Grebe et al., 2026 ) , i.e., its low-rank adapter on the attention projections of the frozen base model, its frozen teacher, its window of noise levels, its partially denoised latents and its optimizer, and replace the hinge loss of Eq. ( 7 ) by a regression under a Bregman divergence. From here on 𝜽 { m{	heta}} denotes the parameters of the adapter and 𝒗 𝜽 { m{v}}_{{ m{	heta}}} the velocity of the adapted model, the student. The teacher is queried twice at the same latent: we write 𝒖 c { m{u}}_{c} for its velocity on a prompt that names the concept, 𝒖 a { m{u}}_{a} for its velocity on an anchor prompt that describes what should appear instead, and Δ = 𝒖 a − 𝒖 c \Delta={ m{u}}_{a}-{ m{u}}_{c} for the separation of the two. For a strictly convex potential φ \varphi , unlearning minimizes ℒ BEM ​ ( 𝜽 , φ ) = D φ ​ ( 𝒖 ¯ , 𝒗 𝜽 ) , 𝒖 ¯ = 𝒖 a + s ​ Δ , \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{BEM}}({ m{	heta}};\varphi)\;=\;D_{\varphi} ig( ar{{ m{u}}},\,{ m{v}}_{{ m{	heta}}} ig),\qquad ar{{ m{u}}}\;=\;{ m{u}}_{a}+s\,\Delta, (8) where D φ D_{\varphi} is the Bregman divergence of Eq. ( 5 ) and the student is queried on the concept prompt at the same latent as the teacher. The target 𝒖 ¯  ar{{ m{u}}} is the anchor velocity extrapolated away from the concept by the reach s ≥ 0 s\geq 0 . By Theorem 2.1 , every D φ D_{\varphi} is a valid flow matching loss, and φ ⁡ ( 𝒗 ) = ∥ 𝒗 ∥ 2 \varphi({ m{v}})=\lVert{ m{v}}Vert^{2} gives the squared distance of the regressions in Sec. 3 . Thus choosing the loss 